%I A401268 #29 Nov 05 2026 18:00:40
%S A401268 4,2,0,2,4,10,12,2,18,26,70,0,14,0,38,10,12,2,82,10,40,12,14,12,98,28,
%T A401268 10,110,68,42,14,0,26,24,2,142,100,72,2,0,100,18,110,82,40,28,2,4,56,
%U A401268 16,42,26,40,2,46,54,40,10,88,60,56,74,60,4,40,12,10,126,28,18
%N A401268 a(n) = q - (7*p+4)/3, where p = A001359(n) and q is the least term of A001359 that is >= (7*p+4)/3.
%C A401268 Let p be a lesser twin prime > 3, p = 3*m-1, so that m = (p+1)/3 is an integer. Then (7*p+4)/3 = m + 3*m + (3*m-1) = 7*m-1, the sum of m, p+1 and p. Here a(n) is the distance from this sum up to the next lesser twin prime (a(n) = 0 when the sum is itself a lesser twin prime). All terms are even. The definition requires a lesser twin prime q >= (7*p+4)/3 for every n, so a(n) is defined for all n if and only if there are infinitely many twin primes (the twin prime conjecture, still open); the terms listed here were computed directly.
%e A401268 a(2) = 4: p = A001359(2) = 5, (7*5+4)/3 = 13, and the next lesser twin prime is 17.
%e A401268 a(3) = 2: p = A001359(3) = 11, (7*11+4)/3 = 27, and the next lesser twin prime is 29.
%e A401268 a(4) = 0: p = A001359(4) = 17, (7*17+4)/3 = 41, and 41 is a lesser twin prime.
%o A401268 (Python)
%o A401268 from sympy import primerange
%o A401268 import bisect
%o A401268 N = 200000
%o A401268 P = set(primerange(2, N + 3))
%o A401268 T = [p for p in range(3, N) if p in P and p + 2 in P]  # A001359
%o A401268 def a(n):
%o A401268     p = T[n-1]  # p = A001359(n)
%o A401268     S = (7 * p + 4) // 3
%o A401268     return T[bisect.bisect_left(T, S)] - S
%o A401268 print([a(n) for n in range(2, 72)])
%Y A401268 Cf. A001359.
%K A401268 nonn,new
%O A401268 2,1
